25":"return","H814":"gift","H758":"Aram","H4639":"work","H5306":"emerald","H948":"fine linen","H7215":"coral","H3539":"ruby","H3063":"Judah","H776":"country/planet","H3478":"Israel","H2406":"wheat","H4511":"Minnith","H6436":"cake","H1706":"honey","H8081":"oil","H6875":"balsam","H1834":"Damascus","H3196":"wine","H2463":"Helbon","H6785":"wool","H6713":"Sahar","H2051":"casks","H187":"Uzal","H6219":"smooth","H6916":"cassia","H7070":"branch","H899":"treachery","H2667":"spread","H7396":"riding","H6152":"Arabia","H5387":"leader","H6938":"Kedar","H3733":"saddle","H352":"ram","H6260":"goat","H7614":"Sheba","H7484":"Raamah","H7218":"head","H1314":"spice","H68":"stone","H3368":"precious","H2091":"gold","H2771":"Haran","H3656":"Canneh","H5729":"Eden","H804":"Assyria","H3638":"Chilmad","H4360":"perfection","H1545":"garment","H1595":"treasury","H1264":"rich apparel","H2256":"Mahalab","H2280":"to saddle/tie","H729":"reinforced","H4819":"market","H7788":"to travel","H4390":"to fill","H3513":"honour","H3966":"much","H4325":"water","H935":"come","H7307":"spirit","H6921":"east","H7665":"to break","H834":"which","H6951":"assembly","H8432":"midst","H5307":"fall","H3117":"day","H4658":"carcass","H6963":"voice","H2201":"outcry","H7493":"to shake","H4054":"pasture","H3381":"to go down","H8610":"to capture","H5975":"stand","H8085":"hear","H2199":"to cry out","H4751":"bitter","H5927":"rise","H6083":"dust","H665":"ashes","H6428":"to wallow","H7139":"to make bald","H7144":"bald spot","H2296":"to gird","H8242":"sackcloth","H1058":"to weep","H4553":"mourning","H5204":"wailing","H6969":"to chant","H4310":"who?","H1822":"destroyed","H3318":"come","H7646":"to satisfy","H6238":"to enrich","H4428":"king","H6256":"time","H4615":"deep","H8074":"destroyed","H8175":"to shudder","H8178":"shuddering","H7481":"to thunder","H6440":"before","H8319":"to whistle","H1091":"terror","H369":"nothing","H5704":"till","H5769":"enduring"},"ccc":{},"cccp":{},"com":[["haydock",2,null,"haydock","Lamentation. Such canticles were usual, and very poetical."],["haydock",3,null,"haydock","Entry, whence merchants may proceed from an excellent harbour to any place."],["haydock",4,null,"haydock","Neighbours of Sidon, Josue xix. 29. (Calmet) — Septuagint, “thy children.” Protestants, “thy builders.” (Haydock) — The description of the Tyrian grandeur, shews their more woeful ruin. (Worthington)"],["haydock",5,null,"haydock","Thee. Hebrew, “all thy ship-boards.” (Septuagint) (Protestants) (Haydock) — St. Jerome has divided (Calmet) leuthim, “decks of the sea,” as yam denotes the sea. (Haydock)"],["haydock",6,null,"haydock","Benches. Septuagint, “temples.” — Italy. Hebrew Cetim. Macedonia. (Bochart) (Calmet) — All distant places were styled islands, (Haydock) when they went by water to them."],["haydock",7,null,"haydock","Linen. Cotton, (Exodus xxv. 4.) used for standards. Septuagint, “for bed coverlets,” or for sails. — Mast. Cleopatra and Caligula were still more sumptuous in their sails. — Elisa, or Elis, famous for purple: yet Tyre was more so."],["haydock",8,null,"haydock","Aradians. Sidon and Arad were then subject to Tyre, and supplied rowers. — Pilots. They studied no other science."],["haydock",9,null,"haydock","Gebal. Septuagint, “Biblos,” which is the same, 3 Kings v. 18. — Furnished. Hebrew, “were in thee to repair thy breaches.” Septuagint, “strengthened thy designs.”"],["haydock",10,null,"haydock","Lybians. Hebrew, “Phut.” They had been expelled by the Cyreneans. Tyre had in her pay the most warlike nations of Persia, &c. Cyrus soon after shook off the yoke of the Medes, and conquered the Lydians. — Hung up, ver. 11. This was very usual, Canticle of Canticles iv. 4., and Isaias xxii. 8. (Calmet)"],["haydock",11,null,"haydock","The Pygmeans. That is, strong and valiant men. In Hebrew Gammadim. (Challoner) — He does not speak of those fabulous men hardly a cubit high. Gomed signifying a “cubit,” has caused them to be styled so here. Septuagint, “guards;” or Symmachus, “Medes.” Ezechiel (xxxviii. 6.) speaks of the Gomerim."],["haydock",12,null,"haydock","Carthaginians. Hebrew, “Tharsis,” in Cilicia; (Genesis x. 4.; Calmet) or distant merchants, who came by sea. (Haydock)"],["haydock",13,null,"haydock","Slaves. Those from Greece were much esteemed. (Calmet) — Alas! thirty thousand Tyrians were themselves thus sold by Alexander [the Great]! (Haydock)"],["haydock",14,null,"haydock","Horses. Those of Sarmatia (Calmet) were in high repute. (Pliny, [Natural History?] viii. 42."],["haydock",15,null,"haydock","Dedan. Septuagint, “Rhodians;” or rather Arabs are meant, ver. 20. They might receive ivory from Ethiopia. — Teeth. Hebrew, “horns or tusks,” which the elephant casts every year. The ivory is less brittle, 3 Kings x. 18. (Calmet) — Ebony; a hard black wood, like horn. (Bochart)"],["haydock",16,null,"haydock","Syrian: always much addicted to commerce. (St. Jerome) — Septuagint read Adam for Aram, as if the traffic in men was meant: (Calmet) “ivory, and to those who brought, thou gavest thy rewards. (16) Men of thy traffic,” &c. (Haydock) — Linen. Hebrew buts, “silk” extracted from the pinna fish, 1 Paralipomenon xv. 27. Silk. Hebrew ramoth, may rather denote unicorns, Job xxviii. 18. (Calmet) — Chodchod. It is the Hebrew name for some precious stone, but of what kind in particular, interpreters are not agreed. (Challoner) — Some say the carbuncle, &c. St. Jerome renders it the jasper, Isaias liv. 12. (Worthington) — Here he confesses he knows not the meaning. (Calmet)"],["haydock",17,null,"haydock","Rosin. Our version generally renders this, balm. (Haydock) — It was much used to heal, Jeremias viii. 22., and Genesis xxxvii. 25."],["haydock",18,null,"haydock","Rich. Hebrew Chelbon; perhaps the city Chelba, Judges i. 31. The kings of Persia used this wine, and planted vines at Damascus on purpose."],["haydock",19,null,"haydock","Dan: the citizens of Peneas, the tribe of Dan was in captivity. Grotius places these nations in Zeilan, (Calmet) or Ceylon. (Haydock)"],["haydock",20,null,"haydock","Seats, such as the Turks still use, or to throw over horses instead of saddles."],["haydock",23,null,"haydock","Haran, or Charę, famous for the residence of Abraham and the defeat of Crassus. — Eden, the province where Paradise was situated."],["haydock",24,null,"haydock","Cords, in boxes, which had then no locks."],["haydock",25,null,"haydock","Sea. Hebrew Tharsis, in Cilicia; or large, and fit for long voyages. Thine were the best. (Calmet)"],["haydock",26,null,"haydock","South. Hebrew kodim, (Haydock) “eastern,” or rather “burning,” here means Nabuchodonosor, who came from the north, (chap. xxvi. 7.; Calmet) or east. The fall of Tyre is described as a shipwreck. (Haydock)"],["haydock",30,null,"haydock","Ashes. They followed the same customs as the Jews. (Calmet) — The latter were ordered to avoid cutting the hair, like them; yet did so, Deuteronomy xiv., and Isaias xxii. 22. (Worthington)"],["haydock",36,null,"haydock","Hissed, through pity and astonishment. (Calmet)"],["challoner",5,null,"challoner","Sea planks.... That is, timber brought by sea to build the city."],["challoner",11,null,"challoner","Pygmeans.... That is, strong and valiant men. In Hebrew, Gammadim."],["challoner",16,null,"challoner","Chodchod.... It is the Hebrew name for some precious stone; but of what kind in particular interpreters are not agreed."]],"au":{"haydock":["George Leo Haydock",0,0,1849,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/07160a.htm"],"challoner":["Richard Challoner",0,0,1781,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/03566a.htm"]}}